// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  dialogue/*.yarn  —  conversations. Yarn Spinner; the game compiles these
//  when it loads your campaign, so there is no tool to run and no build step.
//  Full guide: templates/README.md
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//  TWO RULES, AND THE LOADER ENFORCES BOTH.
//
//  1. EVERY LINE NEEDS A #line: TAG. Without one Yarn invents an id out of a
//     hash of the text - and then editing the words orphans every translation
//     of that line. The tag is lowercase a-z, 0-9 and underscore, and it
//     becomes the last segment of the line's key:
//
//         dialogue.<speaker>.line.<your_campaign_id>.<tag>
//
//  2. THE TEXT BELOW IS YOUR WORKING COPY AND IS NEVER SHOWN. The words a
//     player reads come from locale/<your-id>.csv, in every language
//     including English. Write here to see the shape of the scene; put the
//     finished wording in the CSV. Press L in the dice tray and every line
//     should mangle - one that does not is being read out of this file.
//
//  Each node says who is talking with a 'speaker:' header. Speakers are keyed
//  by CREATURE and never by class: 'wolf', not 'barbarian'. A line can name a
//  different speaker with a '#speaker:imp' tag where two creatures share a
//  scene.
//
//  This template speaks as 'example_companion', which templates/my_classes
//  ships - so the two templates work together and neither leans on a pack
//  that happens to be installed.
//
//  Your campaign must be able to speak as that creature: ship a companion in
//  companions/ or a bark bank in barks/, or depend on a pack that does.

title: example_talk
speaker: example_companion
---
Something down there is breathing. #line:something_is_breathing

-> Ask what it is #line:ask_what_it_is
    Nothing I have a word for. #line:no_word_for_it
-> Say nothing #line:say_nothing
    Suit yourself. #line:suit_yourself
===

// A node with a 'topic:' header is a CAMP SCENE - the conversation by the fire
// on a night that matches. The day picks the topic, and the vocabulary is the
// engine's: loss, boss, bloodied, trouble, wounded, untouched, quiet.
//
// WRITE A QUIET ONE. A night falls back to 'quiet' and to nothing else,
// because every other topic asserts something about a day the companion would
// then be wrong about.

title: example_quiet_night
speaker: example_companion
topic: quiet
---
Nothing tried to eat us today. Sleep. #line:nothing_tried_to_eat_us
===
